The shapes `extract_prose.py` has to lift, and the markup it has to drop.

Expected, and nothing else:
  The title above is one unit; this paragraph is a second; the one below a
  third. A bare ';' ends a unit and a lone '.' ends a unit, and both must,
  because SkoolKit uses the first between a title and its description and the
  second between description paragraphs.

  A braced group is one unit however many rows it spans. An indented ';' row
  carrying no address continues the comment above it - dropping those was what
  cut sentences in half and made every sentence-scoped check read half a
  sentence. A routine link keeps its address, because the address is what the
  density rules count. Every other macro goes, and a table span goes whole.
  A directive between two rows of a group does not break the group.

One thought over three rows, carrying a link to
$ED00 and a register HL that reads as English.

A standalone row is its own unit.

A row whose comment runs on
past a directive and onto an
indented row is one unit.

A table is markup, not prose, and leaves nothing behind.

The sentence after the table is still its own unit.

A cell rendered by  and sized
by 42 keeps only its words.
